{% comment %}
  One shoppable-videos widget: its heading, and one skeleton per video on it.

  The six blocks are identical apart from the layout they pass in, so the
  markup lives here once. `{% render %}` runs in an isolated scope — `block`
  is NOT visible inside a snippet — which is why `widget_id` is passed as a
  parameter rather than read off `block.settings` here.

  The widget comes from an app-data metafield on the app installation, which
  Liquid reaches through the `app` object. It is hidden from the admin and
  readable only by this app.

  ── Why the styles are inline ──

  The skeletons are empty divs, so they collapse to nothing without CSS. Two
  earlier attempts at loading `sje-widget.css` both failed on the storefront:
  `asset_url` (which resolves against the THEME's assets, not the
  extension's), and then the schema's `stylesheet` key. Proven by debug: the
  heading rendered while the skeletons did not, and hand-written inline
  styles on the same markup rendered fine.

  So the sizing lives in `style` attributes, which cannot fail to apply. The
  classes are kept alongside them so `sje-widget.css` can take over — and be
  overridden by a merchant's theme — once the asset is confirmed to load.

  Accepts:
    layout    - one of carousel | grid | story-bar | floating-bubble
                | stacked | masonry
    widget_id - the widget's 6-digit id, from the block's setting
    padding   - large | medium | none, from the block's setting. Controls the
                container's WIDTH (see `sje-widget--pad-*` in the stylesheet),
                which is where it must live: each step has a different value
                on mobile and a `style` attribute cannot hold a media query.
    heading_alignment - left | center | right
    heading_scale     - heading font size as a PERCENTAGE of the standard font
                        size, from the block's setting. 175 means 1.75x.
    heading_color     - blank to inherit the theme's own heading colour
{% endcomment %}

{%- assign sje_key = 'widget_' | append: widget_id -%}
{%- assign sje_widget = app.metafields.shoppable_videos[sje_key].value -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Every size below is a multiple of one of the two standard tokens, never a
  bare number — see CLAUDE.md §3. Held in a variable rather than written out
  each time for two reasons: the expression appears a dozen times, and Liquid
  content in an app extension is capped at 100 KB of SOURCE, so the short form
  is what keeps that budget spendable on logic.

  ⚠️ The fallback literal inside `var()` is not optional. This snippet's own
  note below records two failed attempts to load `sje-widget.css` on the
  storefront, and an undefined custom property makes the whole `calc()`
  invalid — which for a skeleton with no intrinsic height means it disappears.
  With the fallback, a sheet that never loads costs only the mobile step-down.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign sje_sp = 'var(--sje-standard-spacing, 8px)' -%}
{%- assign sje_fs = 'var(--sje-standard-font-size, 14px)' -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Per-layout geometry. Base look is shared by every skeleton.

  `font-size:0;line-height:0` pairs with the `&nbsp;` each skeleton carries:
  themes commonly hide empty elements (`div:empty { display: none }`), and
  these placeholders have no content of their own, so an empty div is dropped
  from layout and both it and its row measure zero however much height is set.
  One character stops the selector matching; zeroing the font keeps that
  character from taking up space.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture sje_item_base -%}
  background:rgba(0,0,0,0.08);border-radius:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 1);font-size:0;line-height:0;
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  ⚠️ Every skeleton states a real HEIGHT (derived, but real).
  `aspect-ratio` alone does not work
  here: these are empty divs, and in a flex row (`align-items: stretch` by
  default) an item sized only by a ratio takes its cross size from a line
  whose height comes from the items — circular, so it resolves to 0 and the
  whole row measures `W x 0`. The rows also set `align-items:flex-start` so
  nothing stretches. Use a ratio only once these hold real content.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Geometry is derived from the spacing token too — a card is `* 30`, a story
  circle `* 9`, a bubble `* 12`, a grid track `* 20`. The multipliers are large
  and not round on purpose: they are exact against the 8px desktop base, so
  nothing moves there, and the whole layout steps down together on a phone.

  `22vw` and the `1fr` below stay as they are: a viewport unit and a fraction
  are already relative, and pinning them to a px token would make them less
  responsive, not more. See CLAUDE.md §3.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- case layout -%}
  {%- when 'grid' -%}
    {%- capture sje_items_style -%}
      display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(calc({{ sje_sp }} * 20),1fr));gap:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 1.5);
    {%- endcapture -%}
    {%- capture sje_item_style -%}
      height:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 30);
    {%- endcapture -%}
  {%- when 'masonry' -%}
    {%- capture sje_items_style -%}
      column-count:4;column-width:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 20);column-gap:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 1.5);
    {%- endcapture -%}
    {%- capture sje_item_style -%}
      height:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 27.5);margin-bottom:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 1.5);break-inside:avoid;
    {%- endcapture -%}
  {%- when 'story-bar' -%}
    {%- capture sje_items_style -%}
      display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 2);overflow-x:auto;
    {%- endcapture -%}
    {%- capture sje_item_style -%}
      flex:0 0 calc({{ sje_sp }} * 9);height:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 9);border-radius:50%;
    {%- endcapture -%}
  {%- when 'floating-bubble' -%}
    {%- capture sje_items_style -%}
      position:fixed;inset-inline-end:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 2);inset-block-end:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 2);z-index:5;
    {%- endcapture -%}
    {%- capture sje_item_style -%}
      width:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 12);height:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 12);border-radius:50%;
    {%- endcapture -%}
  {%- else -%}
    {%- comment -%} carousel and stacked: one row the shopper scrolls. {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- capture sje_items_style -%}
      display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 1.5);overflow-x:auto;
    {%- endcapture -%}
    {%- capture sje_item_style -%}
      flex:0 0 clamp(calc({{ sje_sp }} * 17.5),22vw,calc({{ sje_sp }} * 27.5));height:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 30);
    {%- endcapture -%}
{%- endcase -%}

{%- comment -%}
  A block that cannot find its widget renders NOTHING on the storefront,
  which is right for a shopper but leaves the merchant staring at an empty
  section with no idea why. So in the theme editor — and only there,
  `request.design_mode` — it says which reason applies. The most common by far
  is the first: a deep link can add the block but cannot fill in its settings,
  so a freshly added block has no Widget ID yet.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- if widget_id == blank -%}
  {%- if request.design_mode -%}
    <div class="sje-widget__notice">
      Enter a Widget ID in this block&rsquo;s settings. You can find it under the
      widget&rsquo;s name in the Shoppable Videos app.
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

{%- elsif sje_widget == blank -%}
  {%- if request.design_mode -%}
    <div class="sje-widget__notice">
      No widget found with ID {{ widget_id }}. Check the ID against the one in the
      Shoppable Videos app.
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

{%- elsif sje_widget.enabled == false -%}
  {%- if request.design_mode -%}
    <div class="sje-widget__notice">
      &ldquo;{{ sje_widget.name }}&rdquo; is switched off, so it is hidden from
      shoppers. Turn on Active in the Shoppable Videos app to show it.
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

{%- elsif sje_widget.mediaIds.size == 0 -%}
  {%- if request.design_mode -%}
    <div class="sje-widget__notice">
      &ldquo;{{ sje_widget.name }}&rdquo; has no videos yet, so there is nothing to
      show. Add some in the Shoppable Videos app.
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}

{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The `auto` centres the narrowed container, and has to be inline because
    this element's `style` beats the stylesheet. The block margin is three
    standard spacings, so it tightens with everything else on a phone.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div
    class="sje-widget sje-widget--{{ layout }} sje-widget--pad-{{ padding | default: 'medium' }}"
    style="margin:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 3) auto;"
  >
    {%- if sje_widget.name != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The colour is only emitted when the merchant actually picked one. A
        `color` setting left alone comes back blank, and always writing one
        would stop the heading inheriting the theme's own colour.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The merchant's size feeds a TOKEN, not `font-size` directly, so the
        heading is one more thing derived from the standard scale rather than
        an absolute px that ignores it. The setting is a percentage, so 175
        means 1.75x the standard font size — and it shrinks on a phone with
        everything else, which an absolute px never did.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign heading_mult = heading_scale | default: 175 | divided_by: 100.0 -%}
      <h2
        class="sje-widget__heading"
        style="
          --sje-heading-font-size:calc({{ sje_fs }} * {{ heading_mult }});
          margin-block-end:calc({{ sje_sp }} * 1.5);
          text-align:{{ heading_alignment | default: 'left' }};
          font-size:var(--sje-heading-font-size);
          {%- if heading_color != blank -%}
            color:{{ heading_color }};
          {%- endif -%}
        "
      >{{ sje_widget.name }}</h2>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="sje-widget__items" style="{{ sje_items_style }}">
      {%- for sje_media_id in sje_widget.mediaIds -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          The `&nbsp;` is LOAD-BEARING — do not tidy it away. See
          `sje_item_base` above for why an empty div disappears.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <div
          class="sje-widget__item"
          style="{{ sje_item_base }}{{ sje_item_style }}"
          aria-hidden="true"
        >&nbsp;</div>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
